    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13306",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=3-4m,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is *odd* since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3-4m \\\\  & =(-4m+2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-2m+1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2m+1},$ which is an integer.",
